  "id": "DrugBank_DB06779 - MONDO_grouped_1243_1242",
  "verbalization": "Dalteparin, Heparin, and Adomiparin are all anticoagulants that share a common indication for Purpura Fulminans, a severe form of thrombocytopenic purpura. This shared indication links them to Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation (DIC), a systemic coagulopathy characterized by the formation of microthrombi in various organs. The evidence suggests that these anticoagulants may contribute to the pathophysiology of DIC through their role in modulating coagulation and fibrinolysis. The presence of multiple indirect pathways—each involving a different anticoagulant—indicates a potential mechanism by which these drugs could influence the coagulation cascade, potentially leading to the development of DIC. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of anticoagulant therapy in the management of DIC, though the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated.",
